[]{#sec:preliminaries label="sec:preliminaries"}

All definitions in this section can be found in [@Sagan].

In what follows we use $$\widehat{G}=\{\rho_1,\rho_2,\ldots,\rho_k\}$$ to denote a complete set of inequivalent (complex) irreducible matrix representations of a group $G$, with the convention that $\rho_1$ is the trivial representation. For any $\rho_i \in \widehat{G}$, the map $$\chi_i: g \mapsto \mathrm{Trace}(\rho_i(g)),\;\, g \in G$$ is the *character* of $\rho_i$, and the ratio $$\tilde{\chi}_i(g):=\frac{\chi_i(g)}{\chi_i(e)}$$ is known as the *normalized character* of $\rho_i$ on
